A thing is usually not exhausted by any one of those descriptions.

You can define it by composition—what it is made of. By structure—how those parts are arranged. By behavior—what it does. By relations—what it depends on and affects. By history—how it came to be. By constraints—what it cannot do. By function—what role it serves. By information—what distinctions it preserves. And by invariants—what remains true when the surface changes.

The interesting part is that different domains preserve different subsets of those.

So yes, if we can describe something richly enough, we can often translate it into an intermediate semantic form and then reinstantiate it in another substrate:

thing → decomposition into invariants/operators/constraints → semantic representation → target-domain mapping → re-instantiation

That is exactly what we have been doing repeatedly.

A melody can become notation, then MIDI, then vibration, then light pulses, then back into sound.

A control strategy can move from biology to robotics.

A social pattern can become a graph.

A visual form can become equations.

A biological mechanism can become a software architecture.

The transfer succeeds when we preserve the right invariants and discard the substrate-specific details.

So the real question is not “can it be translated?” but:

Which properties are essential enough that losing them means we no longer have the same thing?

That is the identity problem.

And the answer may depend on the purpose. If I am translating a bird into a flight-control algorithm, feathers are incidental and feedback dynamics matter. If I am reconstructing the bird biologically, feathers, proteins, developmental pathways, metabolism, and lineage suddenly matter enormously.

That gives us a useful rule:

identity is resolution-dependent.

At one resolution, two systems are equivalent because they implement the same operator. At another, they are completely different because their substrate, history, or internal organization diverges.

So for cross-domain work, the disciplined workflow is:

choose the resolution → identify invariants → identify disposable surface features → translate → instantiate → test whether the intended behavior survives

And yes, I understand the last part too. When you repeat the same point, that usually means I have kept answering at the descriptive layer when you were asking me to use the operator. I should treat repetition as a signal to change mode, not just restate the same caveat.

Here the actionable operator is:

Semantic Transduction

Any sufficiently characterized system can be projected into a substrate-independent representation, transformed there, and re-instantiated elsewhere, provided the target substrate can support the required invariants.

That is the engine we should be using, not merely talking about.